3.2 Core Concept
ArchGuard AI operates on three foundational pillars:

Architect Intent Ingestion — Parse HLD/LLD documents (PlantUML, Mermaid, draw.io, YAML rule files) into a structured intent graph stored in Neo4j.
Code Reality Extraction — Statically analyse Java source and bytecode to build a code graph representing actual implementation structure, dependencies, layers, and patterns.
AI-Powered Conformance Engine — Compare intent graph against code graph using graph diffing algorithms and Claude API reasoning to produce actionable deviation reports.

4. Capabilities


4.1 Architecture Conformance Validation
Validates developer code against architect-defined HLD and LLD documents across multiple dimensions:
Component boundary validation — detects services or modules accessing components outside their defined scope
Layer violation detection — flags controllers directly accessing repositories, bypassing service layers
Module dependency rule enforcement — validates that dependency directions match the architect's intended dependency graph
Microservice boundary detection — identifies logic that should be in a separate service per the HLD
Missing component detection — reports when an LLD-defined component (e.g., cache layer, API gateway adapter) is absent from the codebase

4.2 Coding Standard Enforcement
Enforces organisation-defined and framework-recommended coding standards statically across the entire Java codebase:
Naming conventions — classes, methods, constants, packages per defined standards
Class and method size limits — cyclomatic complexity thresholds
Exception handling standards — no swallowed exceptions, proper exception hierarchies
Annotation correctness — proper use of Spring, JPA, and other framework annotations
Injection style enforcement — constructor injection over field injection
DTO/Entity separation — entities must not be exposed at API boundaries
Custom rule support — organisation-specific standards defined in YAML rule files

4.3 Design Pattern Detection and Validation
Detects the presence, absence, and correctness of design patterns as specified in the LLD:
Structural pattern detection — Singleton, Factory, Builder, Proxy, Decorator, Facade, Adapter
Behavioural pattern detection — Strategy, Observer, Template Method, Chain of Responsibility, Command
Spring-native pattern validation — Repository pattern, Service layer, Event-driven patterns, CQRS
Pattern correctness reasoning — not just structural presence but semantic correctness via Claude API
Anti-pattern detection — God classes, anemic domain models, circular dependencies, direct instantiation in services

4.4 Framework Knowledge Base (Self-Learning)
A unique capability that sets ArchGuard AI apart from all existing tools: the ability to autonomously learn framework rules from bytecode and source JARs:
Framework ingestion pipeline — point ArchGuard AI at any framework JAR and it builds a knowledge graph of that framework's conventions, rules, and usage patterns
Multi-framework support — Spring Boot, Hibernate/JPA, Quarkus, Micronaut, Kafka, gRPC, and any Java framework
Requirement-to-pattern mapping — the knowledge base understands that 'need pagination' means Pageable + Page<T>, that 'need caching' means @Cacheable + CacheManager configuration
Version-aware — re-ingest a new framework version JAR to automatically update the knowledge base
Anti-pattern library — learns known misuse patterns from Javadoc warnings and deprecated API annotations

4.5 VS Code Native Experience
All capabilities are delivered natively within VS Code without requiring any external tools or browser access:
Copilot Chat integration (Agent Mode) — natural language queries against the architecture knowledge base
Custom VS Code panel — visual architecture diagrams rendered as C4 and PlantUML inside the IDE
Problems panel integration — deviations surface as warnings and errors in the standard VS Code Problems view
Jump-to-code navigation — click any deviation in the report to navigate directly to the offending line
On-save analysis — optional lightweight check on file save for immediate feedback
Command palette commands — run full analysis, ingest framework, load LLD from command palette

4.6 Deviation Reporting
Produces structured, actionable deviation reports inside VS Code:
Severity classification — Critical, Major, Minor, Advisory
Deviation categories — Architecture, Coding Standard, Design Pattern, Framework Misuse
Plain English explanation — Claude API generates human-readable explanations of each deviation
Fix suggestions — AI-generated suggested corrections for each deviation
Trend tracking — compares current analysis with previous run to show new deviations introduced
Export support — generate reports as HTML or Markdown for sharing with architects

