11. Constraints & Assumptions


11.1 Constraints
Target language is Java only in Version 1.0
Requires local Neo4j installation (Community Edition — free)
Claude API requires internet connectivity and a valid Anthropic API key
Framework knowledge base accuracy depends on availability of source JARs alongside binary JARs
Narrative LLD text extraction (Word/PDF) accuracy is approximately 75–80% — PlantUML/YAML inputs are strongly preferred

11.2 Assumptions
Architects will adopt PlantUML or YAML rule files as the standard HLD/LLD input format
Developers have VS Code with GitHub Copilot (paid plan) installed
Projects use Maven or Gradle as build systems
Neo4j Desktop (free) is installable on developer machines
Teams have access to Anthropic API keys for Claude integration

12. Glossary


Term
Definition
Architecture Drift
The progressive divergence between the intended architecture (HLD/LLD) and the actual implemented code
HLD
High-Level Design — architect document defining system components, boundaries, and interactions at a macro level
LLD
Low-Level Design — architect document defining detailed implementation specifications, patterns, and framework usage
MCP
Model Context Protocol — Anthropic's open standard for connecting AI models to external tools and data sources
Intent Graph
A Neo4j graph representation of the architect's design intent, extracted from HLD/LLD documents
